 {:seon.task/name "stale-sections-are-a-part-of-the-page"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner (2026-10-26): :seon.doc/read as an error's subject is a code smell, and so is the machinery behind it; the database knows exactly what changed, so only changed parts render again and the rest hit the render cache; test it with agents in the REPL, then propose the design, and do not build yet another system. What exists (checked 2026-10-27): `seon.problems/stale-sections` compares form ids recorded at load (:seon.doc/read, `seon.doc/read-map`) with current ones and reads history for meaning (`seon.program/meaning`: :var/doc, :var/contract); a section loaded with no read asks git (`seon.root/wiki-baseline`, the :history effect `seon.fx/wiki-history`, `seon.doc/baseline-plan`, `baseline-reads`, `baseline-failed`; :seon.doc/baseline-unread). Measured in a lab root (412 sections, 2,990 vars, 1,155 citations): 0 sections held a :read, because boot and each watcher batch load docs before source (`my.root/load-kinds!`), so the read is taken against a program that is not there yet. Experiments, in a Seon agent's REPL on a lab store branch: (1) the rule 'a section is stale when a cited var has a datom on its meaning in a transaction after the section's text or review' against today's scan after random edits of a docstring, a contract and a body (the results are exactly the sections citing a var whose meaning changed after them; a body edit makes none); (2) its cost in contracted calls and bytes at 412 sections and at x10 (one eavt seek per citation against today's scan); (3) the same as a part of the page on the one path (view, page, diffs) whose input is ctx values, so an unchanged var is identical? and the part's memo a pointer test; (4) source loaded before docs at boot and per batch so a section written with its code is current. If they bear out, it deletes :seon.doc/read, :seon.doc/baseline-unread, wiki-baseline, baseline-plan, baseline-reads, baseline-failed, read-map, the :history effect, the form-id and history comparison in stale-sections