 {:seon.task/name "what-the-model-knows-is-its-record"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-11-08 (docs/decisions.md#what-the-model-knows-is-its-record-owner-2026-11-08): compact an agent by removing data on its record and the diffs recalculate; no turn 0 that is whole by rule; no new system; a pure function with arguments, not magic; the hiccup's diffs are a detail of the wire, not evals saved on the agent; correct without the cache; a render that changes re-renders the data; time travel shows a point's data with that point's renders. Words from the libraries: a point {:branch :commit :t} (Datahike's commit and t; seon.db/point, seon.db/at-commit), hiccup (what a component answers), diff (editscript's). Waits on Web's rename of :seon.render/html.

What's wrong, measured on a lab root at 7716d15518 (worktree recursive-ctx, through bin/mcp): Seon stores the hiccup on the agent as evals, (my.render/render-ctx) whole and (my.render/render-changes) edits, a copy of derived data, with the base of the next diff in the walk's :shown, process memory. A restart sends the whole hiccup again (8 of 8 leading messages identical, then 3,534 chars, the same value as the first; 14,269 -> 18,609). Removing the whole one ((swap! ctx update :evals dissoc id), a db fact) leaves a 178-char diff against nothing. seon.context/redo-tx exists only to repair those copies.

The change: nothing about the hiccup is stored. seon.render/wire, reading the conversation, places before each turn a call that is never recorded, its arguments the points it stands between, from each turn's :seon.model-call/basis (a required :commit) and the agent's branch: the first turn (my.render/hiccup point), whole; each later one (my.render/diff point0 point1), editscript's edits between the hiccup at the two points (seon.render/component-edits); a turn where nothing moved, none. request's never-recorded call path (the [:render] ref) becomes the only one. Pure: the same points and the same render code give the same bytes, and the agent can make the same call at its REPL. Compaction is removing records or turns: the remaining turns' points are the points, only a diff spanning a removed turn changes, and the cache busts from there. The request draws every point with today's render code, so a render that changes re-renders the history and busts the cache from turn 1, on purpose. Time travel to a point draws it with that point's render code (going back to an eval exists in seon.root; to verify: whether a process forked on that branch runs that commit's renders). A cache keyed by point and render code is an optimization; a miss gives the same bytes.

Measured: the diffs derived from consecutive basis commits equal the stored evals byte for byte over 7 turns, except where a render's code changed (drawn with today's code, as ruled). Cold, 42 turns: a fresh walk per point 375.7M ticks, 1.44 GB, 1.5 s; one walk moved forward 296.6M, of which seon.ctx/moved diffing consecutive commits is 229.3M for 19,985 datoms, 354 of each turn's ~410 being :seon.fn/stats rewritten on the branch every turn (with Optimization). To do with it: skip a turn whose moved datoms touch nothing the hiccup read (flush! answers {}); measure again after the stats leave the record.

Deletes: the stored hiccup evals and seon.turn/show-page!'s insert; my.render/render-ctx, render-changes, redo-changes; seon.context/redo-tx, page-at, render-calls, render-eval?; seon.render/forms-moved where only redo used it, page-call, page-value, the walk's :shown; :seon.eval/page (dropped, no erase needed); the word page in these names; docs/render.md#a-redo-is-an-explicit-bust, and docs/render.md#the-render-is-an-eval rewritten; seon.agent-test/a-redo-is-drawn-from-the-page-as-of-each-t. seon.context/at-t stays (seon.root uses it).

Proofs, red first: a restart with nothing moved sends nothing new, the request after it the one before plus the new eval; removing a turn changes only the diff that spans it; a render changed between two turns re-renders the history with the new code; the request built this way equals today's on a history with no render change; a cold build's cost at 10, 100 and 1,000 turns (seon.cost/proof)."
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-walk-per-view"
  :seon.task/plan "**The principle over every step** (owner, 2026-11-08): \"we shouldn't be doing 1m things if we only need to check 1 or two things and that answers the question. Have common sense and a principal engineers wisdom\"; \"datahike has an internal query cache that we can use\". Each step asks the smallest question that decides it:
- which components rerun: the moved datoms against the walk's read index (live, the transaction's report; cold, a few seeks);
- whether a render's code differs: a walk up from the few changed vars;
- whether a past turn changed: a seek per range it read, stopping at the first hit.

A query repeated on one db value (tabs on one view, one render in two processes) is served by Datahike's own query cache; Seon keeps no second cache of its own besides the shared Datastar patch the owner named.

Owner, 2026-11-08: \"do not split the rendering! … the agent is seeing what the user is seeing\"; \"I want this all done by defining render functions\"; \"Don't invent a new system\"; \"everyone can render their own views and we are reusing them even across processes\"; \"Resolve the problems don't just accept them right away … carry information to them as part of your pull and rendering\"; \"Render functions have access to the current db and can do any queries they need … inject any internal change data … optionally … Confirm that the wire function is also just a render function in the same chain\"; \"optimization … with estimates that if we exceed them we stop and fix them\"; \"account for efficient translation into datastar updates too\"; \"We shouldn't be saving rendered data if we can re-render it from a specific point in time\"; \"The cache isn't to be relied upon to save us if we are doing shit inefficently. I can see caching datastar patches or however we are serving them as it's commonly better for browsers to have multiple tabs open with the same thing being shown and we know when something has changed so it's easy to update.\"

What exists (independent audit, 2026-11-08; docs/render.md):
- One walker (seon.render/walker, flush!), but up to five walks of one agent's tree.
- Four per-component change records.
- A wire reader beside the chain (seon.render/wire, wire-parts, :wire-at) and an HTML writer beside it.
- What the model was shown kept in process memory.
- A turn as one placed unit: each eval resends its turn, O(e) per eval, O(e²) per turn (36,308 bytes for 24,821 chars, one sample).
- Root drawing a branch with main's renders (seon.render/roots-walk-of-a-branch-draws-the-branchs-layout).

The rules:
1. **Every step from the pull to the provider's request is a render function on one walk.** A render declares its inputs in its contract's opts (seon.render/inputs): :seon.ctx/ctx, :seon.db/db (any query it needs, tracked), :seon.render/drawn, and two more, each optional and paid for only by renders that ask:
   - the tree's hiccup, for seon.render/html (HTML) and seon.render.model/openai (the request). It is never a re-read of the tree. Each component's output is its hiccup with its :wire/* marks, cached by the walk like any output. The openai render only assembles the cached outputs into the request, and only when a model call asks: O(changed components) plus an O(turns) assembly of cached parts. html likewise builds the patch from the changed components. Taking the whole tree as one input instead would be O(tree) per eval, a regression on today's wire-parts (Optimization).
   - :seon.render/changes, what moved between two points, computed once per pair of points from the ratom and the db and shared by every render that declares it (rule 6).

   wire-parts, :wire-at, seon.model/request-body's separate reading and the separate HTML writer go. The cold page-at-style draws beside the walk go too: seon.render/what-the-model-knows-is-its-record's (my.render/hiccup point) and (my.render/diff p0 p1) as first written, seon.context/request's appended call, redo-tx and page-at. Context rewrites that task to this shape.
2. **A view is the render of one entity's pull, drawn by commit c's render code.**
   - Which render vars a view draws is in its walk.
   - It is decided through reach, not by each drawn var's own form, so a changed helper it calls counts. Take the vars that differ between the drawing process's program and c (seon.merge's delta between the two commits), and walk up :form/_refs from them until you reach a render var the walk drew: O(callers of what changed). If none is reached, that process draws c.
   - Otherwise a process holding c's program draws it: the agent's own if live, else a fork on the branch at c, alive only while viewed, about 120 MB.
   - So root draws main and users' pulls, and a branch only where its renders match.
   - The delta covers what the branch changed and what main changed since. Never take each render's reach downward (seon.render/render-reach measured 559 MB for seon.print/text, docs/render.md#reactive-renders).
   - Verified by Context: a branch's registered schemas are data on the branch (seon.repl/branch-registry, seon.merge/divergence), so my.schema/register! layouts count.
   - Cost (Optimization, for per-var form reads): about 0.5 M ticks for 50 render vars; the walk-up is O(callers of what changed). STOP > 2 M per draw (then compare one content id over the render namespaces). 31% of last week's 261 merges to main touched render, ctx or context, all our sessions' branches; a Seon agent's task branch rarely does. A fork only for an ended agent's branch whose renders differ, spawned on a view and ended when the last viewer leaves; STOP: more than 2 alive at once, and count how often real views need one before building more for it.
   - The load, once built: about 14 M ticks and 120 MB, once per (branch, commit's program), and a second view of the same commit reuses it. STOP: more than one load per key, more than 2 alive at once, or one started for a commit whose render vars root can draw.
   - Not built: a process on a branch holds main's program plus what it evaluated itself. Drawing c by c's program needs the program-diff load (fork, load the diff from main's image to c, about 0.35 s and 120 MB while viewed; docs/turn.md#the-turn-is-a-number, task seon.reconcile/root-work-is-tasks). That is its own build step, owned there. Until it lands, root drawing a branch whose renders differ is a known gap the page states, not hides.
3. **What each reader needs is data in the pull.**
   - Who views an agent is a fact on the user entity, written on open and close only, never per eval. A page's position (scroll, the turn chosen) is data there too.
   - Whether to draw page-only parts at all is measured once controls go to the agent: if the gap is small, the agent draws everything always, with no viewer condition.
   - Whether an agent has a process is a fact (:seon.process/_agent).
   - What the model was sent at each turn is its basis commit (:seon.model-call/basis).
4. **A live agent's commit carries what it changed.** seon.agent/commit! runs between evals in the agent's own process; its walk flushes there, and the changed components go with the :committed event. That is a cache keyed by the commit drawn: a view that lacks its branch's current commit gets diff(its commit, current), point 6.
   - Prerequisite, before any of this: a turn is a list, so an eval is an append (c ≈ 2 components a commit).
5. **An agent with no process, or a past point, is drawn cold.** A page's lists are windows: the newest N by :seon.ctx/sort-key, a seek (3-5 k ticks a window, measured), never all evals. The model's conversation is never windowed: a cold read for the model (a restart, the judge, a past turn's request) holds the whole conversation, by the owner's ruling of 2026-10-28 (no history budget; docs/render.md#the-models-context-a-cached-prefix-then-the-turn).
6. **Older points are how the walk fills :seon.render/changes,** not a function beside it. A component that declares the input with two points gets what moved between them:
   - a turn of the conversation list asks with turn k-1's basis commit and its own (:seon.model-call/basis :commit, both in its pull), and its output is that turn's hiccup;
   - a tab behind the head asks with the commit it shows and the current one.

   One input, two askers. It is O(moved datoms), never O(history), because datoms-diff skips shared subtrees.
   - Two fixes make it cheap: the diff over the attributes the walk reads (Optimization's measure: 1 M against 7 M on one commit; Context builds the attribute set from the walk's read index), and keeping decoded nodes with leaf-merge candidates in the forks (Optimization: est. 3-5×). Context's measure of today's cold derive: 296.6 M at 42 turns, 229.3 M of it diffing.
   - Its model side is task seon.render/what-the-model-knows-is-its-record (Context, on main 3a613208be), rewritten to this shape. The live walk is a cache keyed by the points. Context's record replay measured 2.5 M ticks, exact against the live walk.
7. **A code change re-renders only the turns it reaches.** A turn is affected if any of its components' render vars is reached by walking up :form/_refs from a changed var (the same walk-up as rule 2), so a helper two calls below a render counts. A turn not reached keeps its bytes without being rendered again. The provider's prefix cache holds up to the first turn whose bytes changed. Turns after it keep their bytes but are billed uncached, the real cost of the owner's \"re-render with the new code\" ruling. A time-travel view draws a past point with that point's renders (rule 2).
8. **Nothing rendered is stored** (owner: \"We shouldn't be saving rendered data if we can re-render it from a specific point in time\"). Hiccup, HTML and a model request are derived from a point and re-rendered when needed. A process may hold them in memory as a cache keyed by the point, never as the record. A restart re-derives (rule 6's cost), and no request bytes are saved.
9. **The page's last step is Datastar's patch, costed like the rest.** The html render's output for a commit becomes one SSE event:
   - each changed element replaced by id (Datastar's morph);
   - a new list member appended after its predecessor;
   - a removed one removed;
   - never a whole container for a change inside it.

   The patch is built from the walk's changed components (rule 4's c), so its bytes are those components' HTML. A tab behind its commit gets the patch for diff(its commit, current), and a tab with no view yet gets one whole page.
   - The one cache the owner names: the patch for a view at a commit, built once and shared by every tab showing that view, keyed by (view, commit) and dropped when the walk says the view moved. seon.web already serializes a patch once and offers it to every tab; this keeps that, keyed by the commit. No other step relies on a cache: every stop line is met cold, with Optimization's minimum work as the target.
10. **In-flight output streams live, the model's reply and an eval's output alike** (owner, 2026-11-08: \"stream live\"; \"Can we get model response streaming too?\"; \"I want live updates to be cheap enough we can live stream them. We should be able to run super fast updates with datastar\").
   - **Where each stream starts:** the model call is root's effect (my.fx :model), so a reply's chunks are in root's process, beside the render proc and the tabs, and go to watching tabs with no hop through the agent. An eval's output is the agent's process's (its stdout), so its chunks come with the agent's events.
   - **The owner's ruling: the channel, not db writes** (2026-11-08): \"So we use flow channels for intermittent updates which have nice drop semantics and pull based waiting and pausing? SO we only write the final value? … it doesn't make any sense to update the db. Make sure we understand the right way to do this I know our agents have written really bad flow code in the past\". As writes, 300 chunks cost 90 M ticks today and 30 M at the minimum on the one writer; as a channel, about 3 M (Optimization). It follows docs/flow.md#idioms-for-seon, \"a fact or a channel\": in-flight chunks are display only, and the final value is the fact.
   - **In-flight data may be lost** (owner, 2026-11-08: \"in flight streaming data can be lost without any serious consequences even if we restart the system or are restoring to apoint in time. The db is the source of truth\"). Nothing in flight is held, rebuilt or written; a restart or a point in time draws what the db holds.
   - **One general stream, no path special to the model** (owner, 2026-11-08: \"I don't want a specialized system for this in case we want to stream other things. How do we do this properly with effects that are writing to channels without complicating the exisitng database pull and render work? Can we get a stable ID for the streaming point to append to and then we can just fire message update data to a channel?\"). The pull, ctx, the ratom and the Reactions are unchanged.
     - **The stable id is an entity already in the db.** An effect that streams belongs to an entity committed before it starts: a model call (seon.turn/act! commits :seon.model-call/id \"<agent>/<n>\" and commits before agent/fx! :model) or an eval (its start is one fact, below). That id is the stream's id.
     - **The render draws the target.** The entity's existing component, while the db holds no final value for it, draws an empty element whose DOM id is the entity's id (seon.render/key-id's rule). That is an ordinary render of db data: no reply yet is what the db says.
     - **Any effect writes to one channel:** `{:stream <id> :agent <key> :text s}`, on the web proc's `:streams` in-port, a sliding buffer. The model worker writes one per SSE line that adds text; an eval's stdout comes over the agent's socket with its events and root's api proc outputs it to the same in-port. Measured in a lab (Jolt Flow, 2026-11-08): a worker fed 300 lines into a web proc held on purpose took all 300 in 3 ms and never waited; the held proc then got the first message and the newest 8 (292-299). So a lagging web proc loses the middle of a stream, never its end, and the commit fills the gap.
     - **The web proc appends.** Each message becomes one Datastar patch-elements event, mode append, selector `#<id>`, its text HTML-escaped (it lands as text nodes; a target styled white-space: pre-wrap keeps its newlines), offer!ed to the tabs whose view shows that agent (never >!!; a full tab channel marks the tab stale, seon.web's loss rule, and its keyframe draws the empty target again, so that tab's text resumes mid-stream). Measured in the built-in browser on Seon's Datastar (v1.0.0-RC.7): appends by id land in order and escaped; an append to an id the page lacks changes nothing and logs a PatchElementsNoTargetsFound warning, which is why tabs are chosen by agent rather than all. The render proc takes no part.
     - **The final value replaces it.** The effect ends and its value is committed as today; the normal render redraws the component from the db, and Datastar's morph of the turn's element by id replaces the target (measured: the streamed element is gone and the committed reply shown). An append still in flight finds no element and does nothing but that warning. A tab opened mid-stream shows the text from then on, and the commit fills the rest.
     - **What the render needs:** today seon.render.ui/reply-hiccup draws nothing for a call with no reply, so there is no target. It draws the empty target (`:wire/for \"page\"`, so the model never reads it) while the call holds neither :seon.model-call/reply nor :seon.model-call/error. To prove on a real run: the target reaches a watching tab before the provider's first line (act! commits the call before agent/fx! :model; the first line takes the provider's latency); a first line that beats it is lost from the live text only.
     - **The model read leaves the step (Context).** Today seon.fx/worker-proc runs seon.model/http-post, whose body loop reads the whole reply inside one step (docs/flow.md hazards 1 and 2). The worker step sends the request and hands the body to an edge reader, the one allowed blocking read (the pattern of seon.fork-server's socket reader), which puts each SSE line on the worker's own in-port (about 64 lines, the reader parking when it is full: back-pressure to the provider, no line lost). The worker's step folds one line, outputs its new text to `:streams` with the call's id (act! adds :agent and :call to the request), and at the last line answers {:value} as today. A socket end or failure is the final line, a typed :seon.model/transport error. The proc's stop closes the body. The edge reader splits lines on the newline byte itself before decoding: measured, http-client's :body-pull (jolt.http.core/read-response with stream-body?) hands pieces as the socket gives them, live (1, 304, 608 ms apart as sent), cut mid-line and inside a UTF-8 character; a newline byte never occurs inside a multi-byte character, so decoding whole lines is exact.
     - Open with the owner: whether the fold also extracts the tool call's growing message-md and each eval's code as their own streams (Web's pick), or the stream shows the raw argument JSON until the commit.
   - **The eval's start is one fact; its output is committed once when it ends.** Committing each chunk was rejected (about 0.3 M ticks a chunk, and a moved datom in every later diff).

Cost proofs: the minimum each step should take, and a stop line at 2× it (Optimization, 2026-11-08; about 40 k ticks a ms; per turn m ≈ 56 moved datoms once jolt/fn-stats-at-end lands, c ≈ 2 touched components, b a few KB). Our Datahike fork keeps no transaction log, so a cold read diffs commits, made cheap by Optimization's fork work.
- **A live turn:** the transaction's own report holds its m datoms, so there is no diff. Match them against the walk's read index (O(m log r), about 10 k), rerun c components (30-50 k each), diff their forms (about 20 k) and print b bytes. Minimum about 0.1 M a turn. STOP 0.3 M.
  - Waste to remove: today's measured turn (flush plus read, 333-499 k, docs/render.md) is 3-5× this. Find which components rerun beyond the touched c. That is a task now, owned by Web (the walker).
- **One new eval:** reruns its component and its turn list's append; the commit carries under 5 KB. STOP: e=100 > 2× e=10.
- **The Datastar patch for one new eval:** one SSE event of one append, its bytes ≤ 1.2× that eval's HTML (expect about 1.05×). STOP: e=100 > 2× e=10, or any event carrying a whole container for a change inside it. seon.web/patch-event names the smallest changed element's id.
- **Shared patches:** N tabs on one view cost one patch build plus N sends. STOP: a patch built more than once per (view, commit).
- **The browser's morph** (idiomorph is O(nodes of its target)): DOM nodes morphed per patch ≤ the changed element's own nodes, never its container's, flat at e=10 and e=100 (counted in the built-in browser). Patch-to-paint time is measured and shown, never a red line (AGENTS §5).
- **Streaming:** per message, the fold (3-5 k ticks) plus one SSE frame per open tab (1-2 k), O(message bytes), with no render, no ratom and no db work; committed datoms per reply or eval output = its final form + O(1). STOP: message 300 > 2× message 10, or > 20 k with one tab. Proofs, red first: a tab opened mid-stream shows the text from then on and, after the commit, exactly the committed reply with no duplicated text; 300 messages into a web proc held slow on purpose (a test env holding its step, never a sleep) never make the worker's steps wait; a stream for an id no tab draws costs one offer! per tab and changes nothing; an append that arrives after the commit changes nothing.
- **The push against root's branch-walk!:** measured per eval commit on one real agent. STOP: the push ≥ 25% of branch-walk!, then keep branch-walk!.
- **A cold advance by one turn** (a past point, a lagging tab, the changes input): the diff of two commits over the attributes the walk reads, at 1-2 k a moved datom with decoded nodes cached and candidates from the leaf merge (about 0.1 M), plus a live turn's work. Minimum about 0.2-0.3 M. STOP 0.6 M a turn and 2 k a moved datom (today 10.7 k: decode and candidate waste, Optimization's).
- **The changes input:** one diff per pair of points, about 0.1 M. STOP 0.3 M, and never computed twice for one pair.
- **A tab k commits behind:** one diff over the read attributes between its point and the head (O(unshared nodes in the range)), never k diffs, then a live turn's work. Expect under 1 M at k=10 and under 5 M at k=100. STOP 2× that.
- **A restart** (rebuild the next request):
  - Draw point T cold once. The request is printed anyway, so this is the floor, growing with the conversation at about 0.1 M a turn's component.
  - For each earlier turn, ask one question: did any range a component of that turn's request read differ between point k-1 and point k? Answer it with two seeks on the current index, one at each point. When the leaf holding the range has the same node address at both points, the range is unchanged and no datom is read: about 5 k a range. The model only ever saw each turn's basis state, so this is exact. Not a history seek for a tx window: the history index orders a range's versions by value then tx, so that scans every version, O(T²) for a restart (Optimization). Expect about 20 M at T=100 (20 ranges a turn × 5 k + loading each commit about 0.08 M + one cold draw about 10 M). STOP 40 M.
  - Draw only those turns at their own points (about 0.15 M each).
  - Expect 12-15 M at T=100 and 120-150 M at T=1,000. STOP: above 2× one cold draw at point T, or any turn advanced whose read datoms didn't move.
  - Advancing one walk through every turn (about 54 M at T=100 today, 34 M after fixes) is the waste this avoids. No stored copy, ever (rule 8).
- **The openai render per eval:** O(changed components) plus O(turns) assembly of cached outputs, run only at a model call. STOP: at T=100 > 2× at T=10, more than today's wire-parts on the same agent (measure both before deleting it), or a rerun at an eval no model call reads.
- **A code edit:** re-renders no turn it doesn't reach. Proof, red first: change a helper two calls below a render, and only the turns it drew re-render.
- **A cold page draw at 1,000 evals:** ≤ 1.5× the same at 100, window fixed.
- **A turn's own work:** datoms a turn moves that aren't the turn's own: ≤ 5% (the fn-stats class).
- **Request bytes per turn:** growing no faster than the turn's new content.
- Plus correctness proofs, red first:
  - a dropped push still shows the branch's commit;
  - a restart with nothing moved sends the model nothing;
  - a value changed at one turn and changed back at a later one, then a restart, gives the same request bytes as the turns sent;
  - compaction changes only the diff spanning a removed turn;
  - a user's page holding an agent's view draws that agent's components once;
  - a branch that changed a render is drawn with its renders.

Owners:
- Web: rules 1 (the openai and html renders as walk components, with Context), 2's check, 3's viewer facts, 4, 5, 7, 9, and the turn as a list first.
- reconcile's task (seon.reconcile/root-work-is-tasks): the program-diff load that rule 2 needs.
- Context: rule 6's model side and attribute set, the openai render's content (with Web), the rewrite of seon.render/what-the-model-knows-is-its-record to rule 1's shape, and B's proposal to the owner.
- Optimization: the diff in the forks and seon.db (decode, candidates, attribute restriction), and the cost proofs' numbers.
- Web also: the walker's 3-5× flush waste (components rerun beyond those touched).

Order:
1. Context's seon.context split (into seon.ctx, seon.ctx.search, .status, .wire, .session), next as agreed.
2. The turn as a list.
3. The model-render fold (planned).
4. Rule 1's renders, with Context's task rewrite.
5. The push with its 25% stop line.
6. Rule 2's check.
7. The diff fixes in parallel.

